  "id": "Compound::DB00524 - Gene::763",
  "verbalization": "Metolazone is implicated in the relationship with Ca5a through multiple indirect pathways. The first pathway links Metolazone to Ca5b via binding, which in turn participates in the reversible hydration of carbon dioxide. This process is then associated with Ca5a, suggesting a potential functional interaction. In a second pathway, Metolazone binds to Ca7, which also participates in the same biochemical process, further reinforcing the connection to Ca5a. A third pathway introduces a more indirect route, where Metolazone is included in an increased diuresis, which encompasses Chlorthalidone. Chlorthalidone, in turn, binds to Ca5a, providing an additional layer of evidence for the relationship. The integration of these pathways suggests that Metolazone may influence Ca5a through a shared biochemical mechanism involving the reversible hydration of carbon dioxide, potentially through the action of a thiazide diuretic. The evidence supports a mechanism where Metolazone indirectly affects Ca5a via a chain of interactions involving diuretic compounds and their participation in a common metabolic process.",
